The gene that encapsulates the human enzyme Hefe (Heparanase-2) is HPSE2, located on chromosome ([]);
However the disorder you're referring to is likely MUCOLIPIDOSIS III or Pseudo-Hurler(polydystrophy), but more specifically the condition that fits or closely resembles to the description of children appearing as if they are crying when they smile due to an autosomal recessive disorder is MUCOLIPIDOSIS II  closely related to the HPSE2 gene's neighbor,  the GNPTAB gene and also the GNPTG gene, both located on chromosome  Typically the disorder that involves the appearance of a child crying when smiling is associated with MUCOLIPIDOSIS III alpha/beta, caused by mutations in the GNPTAB gene, located on chromosome Nevertheless, another disorder, MUCOLIPIDOSIS II, caused by mutations in the GNPTAB gene, presents similar symptoms, including an unusual facial expression.

However further research indicates that the disorder referred collectively as MUCOLIPIDOSIS III alpha/beta is more closely associated with the appearance of children crying when smiling and this is caused by mutations in the GNPTAB and GNPTG genes but more specifically the disorder that presents with the distinct facial features is associated with the gene that lies in proximity to HPSE2, namely the GNPTG gene which is located at  It is worth noting that HPSE2 is located at Chr10:10q24-q25.
